WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:13.890
What kind of curve is uniquely
defined by these two points? A

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:14.400
line.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:18.480
How about three points? A
circle.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:23.040
This basic geometry is ancient
history. Euclid 

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.200
proved it more than two thousand years ago  

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.067
in his seminal math treatise "Elements." 

00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:33.947
Now try to figure out the shape of a curve that passes through a million points in a 1,000 dimensions 

00:00:33.947 --> 00:00:36.074
and is super twisty. What would that look like?

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:40.080
 Although daunting, finding a solution to this problem is

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.800
fundamental to probing deeper
principles of math. And the

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.530
solution also holds the
potential to improve digital

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:49.410
data storage, the security of
your crypto wallet, and the

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.460
privacy of electronic
communications. It's called the

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:57.060
interpolation problem. And two
young mathematicians, Eric

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:00.750
Larson and Isabel Vogt of Brown
University have solved it.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:05.550
The interpolation problem asks,
If you fix some general

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:09.120
collection of points, when is
there a curve of some specified

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.530
type through those points?

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.860
In search of answers, the pair
chipped away at the problem for

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:14.460
years,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.390
We started out tackling cases in
small dimensional spaces to try

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:22.290
to get our hands dirty and see
the sort of techniques that we

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:27.300
would need. And then I think
around 2019 or so we came up

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.120
with a good idea that we thought
might help us solve the whole

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:30.750
problem.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.930
Along the way, their
collaboration took on a whole

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:34.770
new dimension,

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.930
Well, we got married in 2017.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:41.280
Much of the groundwork for a
potential solution was laid out

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:45.210
150 years ago by German
mathematicians, Alexander von

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:46.680
Brill and Max Noether

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.010
Before you can ask what
properties does a given type of

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:54.150
curve have, you need to first
answer the question of what

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.500
types of curves are there?

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.160
The theorem predicts what types
of curves can exist based on

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.010
three properties. The first is
dimension,

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.620
You can think about a curve in
actual physical space, that

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.380
would be a three dimensional
space. But we don't constrain

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.510
ourselves to two dimensions or
three dimensions.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.330
The second quantity is the
degree of a curve.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:22.140
So you should think, here, like
how twisty or wobbly the curve

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:22.800
is.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.000
The third is the genus or number
of holes a curve has.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:32.490
Curves themselves, have a sort
of topological property. But if

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.590
you were to look at the
solutions over the complex

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:37.500
numbers, then it's going to look
like something that's two

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.600
dimensional. So it could look
like a sphere. Or it could look

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.810
like the surface of a doughnut.
Or it could look like the

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:50.940
surface of a two-hole doughnut.
In general, it can have any

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.140
number of holes.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:57.300
It wasn't until the 1980s that
modern math offered a proof of

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.780
Brill-Noether. And this opened
doors to the interpolation

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:04.020
problem. Larson and Vogt, we're
determined to solve the problem

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.030
for all possible types of
curves.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.790
We had to come up with three
different ways of breaking the

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:12.750
curve into simpler curves. So we
take some curve, it's

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:16.770
complicated, it's very wobbly.
So we take this wobbly curve,

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.630
and we break it in three
different ways.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:22.560
By breaking down the complex
curve in multiple steps, Larson

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.080
and Vogt were able to get
something simple enough that

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.090
they could attack it with their
bare hands.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.240
We knew how slippery it was, how
you really had to work hard to

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.490
get everything to match up,

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.280
Larson and Vogt's proof
confirmed the interpolation

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.460
problem in all cases, showing
that curves will always

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.760
interpolate through the expected
number of points with only four

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.720
exceptions, which they also
explained. The couple's proof is

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.260
a powerful tool for exploring a
whole new class of ideas.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.440
Bit of a surprise to finally complete it,

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:56.190
you know, it's you've just been
thinking, oh, this is what I do

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.830
is I work on this problem, but
then you're like, wow, it's

00:03:58.830 --> 00:03:59.330
done.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:08.700
More than 2,000 years ago, the
Greek mathematicians Zenodorus

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:11.820
argued that a sphere is the
optimal shape of a single

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:16.500
bubble. This may seem obvious,
but it took until the late 19th

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:19.830
century to prove it
mathematically. What about a

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:21.630
cluster of multiple bubbles?

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.650
Researchers study bubbles to
both better understand their

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:28.590
geometric properties and
potentially improve everything

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.680
from computer algorithms and
biological cell models to

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.970
firefighting foams. For decades,
mathematicians have sought the

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.020
optimal configuration of larger
bubble clusters. But it turns

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.830
out the solution is one of the
hardest problems in geometry.

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:48.810
The bubble problem can be stated
simply: What configuration of

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:52.140
bubbles encloses a given number
of volumes while minimizing

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:53.010
surface area.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.280
And the key point is that the
surfaces are allowed to, like, if I

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.310
smashed my bubbles together I'm
allowed to save the surface

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.350
that's between them. I mean,
it's an optimization problem.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.810
And that's such a universal kind
of problem with all kinds of

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:04.530
applications.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:08.010
From our own experience of
blowing bubbles, we know that

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:11.730
larger clusters of bubbles tend
to form tight clumps rather than

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:15.090
a long chain. But how can we
know whether these formations

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.530
are actually optimal?

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:21.210
In the 1990s, John Sullivan, now
of the Technical University of

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.640
Berlin, formed a guiding
conjecture,

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:27.030
He described what we now call a
standard bubble or a standard

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:29.730
bubble cluster. They're spheres.
They meet at 120 degree angles.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:31.680
They all touch. There could be
lots of different ways to

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.530
achieve that. It turns out that
that's not the case.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:38.190
Sullivan's conjecture states, if
the number of volumes you want

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:41.430
to enclose is at most one
greater than the dimension,

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.490
there's one cluster
configuration, that's the best.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:47.280
There's basically one way to
achieve this and his conjecture

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.140
was that this way is the optimal
way.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:53.190
To visualize Sullivan's optimal
cluster place four points

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:57.870
equidistant on a sphere. Inflate
the points into bubbles or disks

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:01.950
on the surface until they bump
into each other. Keep inflating

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:05.490
them until the entire sphere is
covered. We end up with a

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:10.380
symmetric cluster that all touch
and meet at 120 degree angles.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.310
Now place the sphere on a flat
plane. Add a light at the north

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:18.150
pole to cast a two dimensional
shadow of three bubbles. Rolling

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:20.760
the sphere around creates
clusters of Sullivan's shadow

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:24.755
bubbles that can enclose any
three areas. 

00:06:24.839 --> 00:06:29.700
By 2002, mathematicians had proved
Sullivan's conjecture for the

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:33.510
double bubble. And at this
point, some speculated another

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:36.660
100 years would pass before a
proof for three bubbles could be

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:41.370
found. But then Emanuel Milman
of Technion University, and Joe

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:44.310
Neeman of the University of
Texas, Austin entered the

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:44.810
picture.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:47.490
So what we were working on was
sort of the probabilistic

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:50.370
version of this problem. And we
were interested, actually,

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.110
originally, we were just we
would have been happy to do

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:55.020
double bubble at the beginning.
We were like, you know, we have

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:58.140
to come up with something new.
Right. And so we came up with

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:58.920
some new things.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:02.670
After some success solving the
analogous probabilistic bubble

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:05.670
problem, the pair hit a snag
transferring their new

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:07.650
techniques to the geometric one.

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:12.030
So we were stuck on this for a
long time. And definitely a

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:14.490
breakthrough came when we
actually, we kind of gave up to

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:15.060
be honest.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.410
Milman and Neeman realized that
if you give bubbles, one extra

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:23.250
dimension of space, you get a
bonus: The best bubble cluster

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:26.100
will have mirror symmetry across
a central plane.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.360
We were like, okay, let's give
up on what we were trying to do

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.510
and see what we can get from the
symmetry. And then as soon as we

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:36.900
had that symmetry that, you
know, that symmetry combined

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:39.240
with what we had been working on
for the previous three years,

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:42.090
all of a sudden, like then we
were able to move on from there.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:45.360
But Milman and Neeman's proof
of Sullivan's conjecture for

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.600
triple bubbles in dimensions
three and up is not the end of

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:49.200
the story.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:52.290
We're stuck at a maximum of five
bubbles right now. But I feel

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:55.350
like it's just like a tiny
little thing that we are not

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.600
seeing and then that problem
will go away.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:06.420
Take a set of points and start
randomly connecting them with

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:13.440
lines. At what point will an
interesting pattern emerge? Like

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.360
a triangle or a Hamiltonian
cycle, a chain of lines that

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.410
passes through each point
exactly once. In a branch of

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:25.710
math called combinatorics,
graphs like these are valuable

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:28.860
tools for probing the inner
workings of complex systems.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:32.190
A graph is just another name for
a network.

00:08:32.309 --> 00:08:35.609
Networks are everywhere like
Facebook network. You can think

00:08:35.609 --> 00:08:38.459
about traffic networks, you
know, or business networks and

00:08:38.459 --> 00:08:39.059
things like that.

00:08:39.809 --> 00:08:43.139
But real life networks can be
huge and hard to study. So

00:08:43.139 --> 00:08:46.169
mathematicians use random graphs
to model their properties.

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:50.700
In the random graph theory,
finding thresholds is a central

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:55.170
subject. People worked on
finding threshold for each of

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:56.490
interesting properties.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:08:59.670
Interesting properties can
emerge as you increase the

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:03.510
density of lines in a random
graph. The sudden moment of

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:07.590
change is defined by a
threshold. In nature thresholds

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:10.380
are responsible for abrupt
transformations and the

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:12.240
emergence of complex patterns.

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:14.970
But it turns out that
determining threshold is

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:16.080
extremely difficult.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.280
Instead, mathematicians use a
method to arrive at a ballpark

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:20.790
figure.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:24.240
There is a very natural lower
bound to the threshold that we

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:27.810
call the expectation threshold.
The expectation threshold turns

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:31.020
out to be much easier to
estimate or determine.

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:36.330
In 2006, mathematicians Jeff
Kahn and Gil Kalai posed their

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:39.570
expectation threshold
conjecture. It states that the

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:42.930
gap between the expectation
threshold and the real threshold

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:47.640
is at most a logarithmic factor,
but sometimes smaller, or even

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:48.540
nonexistent.

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:53.400
The Kahn-Kalai conjecture just
gives one simple solution to

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:57.030
find out the location of the
threshold for many, many

00:09:57.030 --> 00:10:02.790
interesting properties. So it's
just so powerful, and it was

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.580
hard to believe that this
conjecture is true.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:10.890
That was until two
mathematicians at Stanford,

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:14.730
Jinyoung Park and Huy Pham,
stumbled onto an elegant six

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:17.310
page solution after a long
sleepless night.

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:22.410
It really came about by really
not working or aiming directly

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:24.090
at the Kahn-Kalai conjecture.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:28.830
It was a surprise that we found
out another way.

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:32.610
The pair were instead working on
several related conjectures.

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:35.790
Like, you know, you're like
taking a walk that you aim at a

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:39.510
seemingly very different and
distant destination, but on the

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:43.860
way, you are suddenly able to
find some very surprising hidden

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:44.360
beauty.

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:47.640
A key breakthrough was the
realization that a method

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:51.090
developed in the pursuit of one
conjecture could be used in a

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:54.060
different manner to solve
Kahn-Kalai. The solution

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:57.300
utilized a mathematical object
called a cover.

00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:01.350
You can think about a cover as
essentially a kind of necessary

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:05.790
condition, a witness to the fact
that the network satisfies the

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:06.360
properties.

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:09.960
Park and Pham employed an
algorithm to sample subsets of a

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:12.150
random graph to home in on the
cover.

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:17.520
In the end, we verify this cover
is small, in particular, so that

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:19.320
is exactly how we prove the
conjecture.

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:23.430
Park and Pham's concise proof of
Kahn-Kalai is expected to lead

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:24.690
to new breakthroughs.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:28.230
This would allow us to get a
much better handle on complex

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:31.950
properties of networks and this
is certainly usefull in many

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:35.040
applications not just in math
or in probabilistic

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:37.560
combinatorics, because networks
are involved everywhere.

